ailite-schedule-first.patch — against the uploaded cargo-master
(af373f761aa4e42b093c2e453078fd73c8ce54dd).

Status: written and reasoned against source, NOT compiled — building cargo
master needs roughly an hour on this 1-vCPU sandbox. Treat as a starting
point. The one API assumption: `unit.pkg.name()` returns an InternedString
with `.as_str()` (the accessor pattern used elsewhere in this file).

Why it works (source refs): src/util/dependency_queue.rs,
queue_finished() — priority(n) = cost(n) + sum of cost over all transitive
DEPENDENTS of n; dequeue() picks max priority. So boosting the cost of
package X's units raises the priority of X *and of every node X depends
on*: cargo compiles toward X first, then the rest of the graph. The
mechanism composes correctly with no other changes. Upstream's own comment
at the patch site says per-unit cost hints are the intended future use.

--- a/src/compiler/job_queue/mod.rs
+++ b/src/compiler/job_queue/mod.rs
@@ -468,11 +468,24 @@
         // For now we use a fixed placeholder value for the cost of each unit, but
         // in the future this could be used to allow users to provide hints about
         // relative expected costs of units, or this could be automatically set in
         // a smarter way using timing data from a previous compilation.
-        self.queue.queue(unit.clone(), job, queue_deps, 100);
+        //
+        // ailite: CARGO_SCHEDULE_FIRST="pkga,pkgb" boosts the named packages'
+        // units. Priority is own cost + cost of all transitive dependents
+        // (see DependencyQueue::queue_finished), so boosting a unit also
+        // prioritizes its entire dependency chain: the build is driven toward
+        // the named packages first — fail-fast ordering under external
+        // (e.g. agent) control. A production version would plumb this through
+        // config instead of reading the env per unit.
+        let cost = match std::env::var("CARGO_SCHEDULE_FIRST") {
+            Ok(list) if list.split(',').any(|p| p.trim() == unit.pkg.name().as_str()) => {
+                1_000_000
+            }
+            _ => 100,
+        };
+        self.queue.queue(unit.clone(), job, queue_deps, cost);
         *self.counts.entry(unit.pkg.package_id()).or_insert(0) += 1;
         Ok(())
     }
